# I Weep

Valluvar asked, “What use is learning if one does not worship the good feet of perfect wisdom?” Finding this true in my heart, I searched all directions for a worthy teacher. I saw an attractive young speaker of thirty years. Drawn by saffron robes, I became a devotee and head of a hill monastery, a servant of Mahadeva.

Hearing his song that he would spread Manikkavasagar's Tamil, remove impurity, and make goodness flourish, I transferred my property and entered the monastery. Told that the name Ilavazhagan was unsuitable near God, I became Manimozhiyar and a slave of the monastery. I travelled with the ascetic, listened to his words, and performed his service, worshipping him as red fruit, classical Tamil, and treasure of virtue.

He said he would drive out false speech, summon the true path, and become an ornament to Tamil Nadu. I clasped his feet. Time passed; devotees sang sacred praise of the virtuous man who had entered the Saivite way. I drank honey-flowing Tamil without then knowing my confusion.

Now I weep. The cloth is saffron, but the thought leaps toward domination. The hand rolls rudraksha beads, while the rolling eyes seek women. Many arrive praising him as an ocean of grace; he beats and enslaves them, frightens them in rage, bewilders them, and calls human beings cattle.

He found ways to pluck fruit and built a mansion for the banana grove. A message reached my ear; I did not accept even a grain of it. Because I possessed eyes, I saw - alas. A servant said the Lord called me. I went slowly to meet the “true one,” seeking his room in the old monastery to offer counsel.

Alas, what I saw! The lord who came to teach grace and a base woman who came to seize wealth spoke with their eyes and sweated together. I trembled, feared, and withered. Is this virtue, the way of grace, moral discipline, or the Saivite path? Is this mansion a chamber of petty pleasure?

My heart urged me to ask whether he had learned to throw cremation-ground ash into people's eyes, what he cast into darkness and called great light, and whether saffron was worn merely to find the bedchamber. But my tongue faltered.

Thinking on this outward disguise, I removed the costume, left the forest of killing tigers, and came to a place of learning.

---NOTES---
Anna's note calls this a story rather than a finished poem: a youth deceived by a hypocritical religious figure discovers the truth, discards saffron, enters family life, and becomes a student of virtue.

